Yes 👍 You are on the Update Credential page, and the credential is already configured.

I can see your ID is:

cag-website-v2-ci-encryption-key

That's fine. Use this exact ID in the Jenkinsfile.

Step 1 — Click Save

At the bottom of the screen, click:

Save

You don't need to reveal or change the concealed secret.

Step 2 — Modify your Jenkinsfile

In your local project:

CAG_Website_v2
└── CI
    └── Jenkinsfile

Find:

stage('Start Backend Test Container') {
    steps {

        sh '''
            set -e

            docker run -d \
                --name "${BACKEND_CONTAINER}" \
                --network "${CI_NETWORK}" \
                -e ENVIRONMENT=production \
                -e DB_HOST=postgres \
                -e DB_PORT=5432 \
                -e DB_NAME="${POSTGRES_DB}" \
                -e DB_USER="${POSTGRES_USER}" \
                -e DB_PASSWORD="${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}" \
                -e DB_SCHEMA=cag_new \
                -e DATABASE_URL="postgresql+psycopg2://${POSTGRES_USER}:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/${POSTGRES_DB}" \
                "${BACKEND_IMAGE}:${GIT_COMMIT}"
        '''
    }
}

Replace that whole stage with:

stage('Start Backend Test Container') {
    steps {

        withCredentials([
            string(
                credentialsId: 'cag-website-v2-ci-encryption-key',
                variable: 'ENCRYPTION_KEY'
            )
        ]) {

            sh '''
                set -e

                echo "=============================================="
                echo "START BACKEND TEST CONTAINER"
                echo "=============================================="

                docker run -d \
                    --name "${BACKEND_CONTAINER}" \
                    --network "${CI_NETWORK}" \
                    -e ENVIRONMENT=production \
                    -e ENCRYPTION_KEY="${ENCRYPTION_KEY}" \
                    -e DB_HOST=postgres \
                    -e DB_PORT=5432 \
                    -e DB_NAME="${POSTGRES_DB}" \
                    -e DB_USER="${POSTGRES_USER}" \
                    -e DB_PASSWORD="${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}" \
                    -e DB_SCHEMA=cag_new \
                    -e DATABASE_URL="postgresql+psycopg2://${POSTGRES_USER}:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/${POSTGRES_DB}" \
                    "${BACKEND_IMAGE}:${GIT_COMMIT}"

                echo ""
                echo "Backend container started:"
                docker ps --filter "name=${BACKEND_CONTAINER}"

                echo ""
                echo "Backend image:"
                docker inspect \
                    "${BACKEND_CONTAINER}" \
                    --format '{{.Config.Image}}'

                echo ""
                echo "Backend container started successfully."
            '''
        }
    }
}

The important addition is:

withCredentials([
    string(
        credentialsId: 'cag-website-v2-ci-encryption-key',
        variable: 'ENCRYPTION_KEY'
    )
])

and:

-e ENCRYPTION_KEY="${ENCRYPTION_KEY}"

This keeps the secret out of your GitHub repository.

Step 3 — Save and commit

After editing:

git status

Then:

git add CI/Jenkinsfile
git commit -m "Fix backend CI encryption key"

Then:

git push origin Dev
Step 4 — Jenkins

Jenkins should detect the new Dev commit and start another build.

Do not manually put the secret into the Jenkinsfile.

Your flow will now be:

